    model: 'The Controlled Substances Act places drugs in schedules according to their accepted medical use and their potential for abuse and dependence, so that drugs with greater risk receive stricter controls. A Schedule II opioid such as oxycodone has high abuse potential: it needs a written or electronic prescription, cannot be refilled, and is tightly tracked. A Schedule IV benzodiazepine such as lorazepam has lower abuse potential and may be refilled up to five times within six months. Facilities prevent diversion with secure storage in locked or automated dispensing cabinets, routine counts at shift change, witnessed waste of unused portions, review of dispensing reports, PDMP checks and prompt reporting of discrepancies.',
